@page "/blogs/getting-started-with-aspire-in-dotnet"
@using SharedModels
@using BaseComponents
@inherits BasePage

<Content FileName=@nameof(GettingStartedWithAspireInDotNet)>
    <ContentBody>
        <What>
            <p>
                Your application needs an API, a UI, a database, and a handful of background services.
                Why does starting work require a checklist of terminals? <Highlight>Aspire</Highlight>
                lets you describe those resources and their connections in code, then launch the
                application from one <Highlight>AppHost</Highlight>.
            </p>
            <p>
                A small .NET catalog makes the Aspire resource model concrete: it connects a
                PostgreSQL database, an API, and a Blazor UI. You will see how resource references,
                startup dependencies, service discovery, and the dashboard fit together. You can
                use the same approach to add Aspire to an existing application, without rebuilding
                it around a starter template. The
                companion article uses this same application for
                <a class="underline" href="/blogs/distributed-testing-with-aspire-and-playwright-in-dotnet/">distributed testing with Aspire and Playwright</a>.
            </p>
        </What>

        <Why>
            <p>
                Starting a multi-service application by hand means remembering which database,
                containers, and supporting tools must run first, then matching each process to the
                right address. With around <Highlight>30 resources</Highlight>, one missed dependency
                can turn into a connection error or timeout that looks like an application bug.
            </p>
            <p>
                Repeating that checklist across a team makes onboarding and troubleshooting harder:
                each developer must know which terminals, settings, and startup order to reproduce.
                Aspire lets a development environment start .NET projects, container images,
                databases, UIs, and supporting tools together; pressing F5 runs that local graph,
                not a production deployment.
            </p>
            <p>
                A versioned resource graph, runtime service addresses, and one place for logs and
                traces reduce that setup drift. The steps below turn the startup checklist into an
                explicit AppHost while keeping production deployment and operations separate.
            </p>
        </Why>

        <How>
            <h4>1. Add Aspire to your existing solution</h4>
            <p>
                Start from your team's existing repository; a starter template is optional.
                Install the .NET SDK and runtime supported by your application, a compatible
                <a class="underline" href="https://aspire.dev/get-started/install-cli/">Aspire CLI</a>,
                and a supported container runtime such as Docker Desktop. Clone the repository,
                follow its normal local configuration process, and obtain secrets from the team's
                approved local secret store or environment—not from committed files. Start the
                container runtime before launching the graph.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-powershell">
Set-Location path\to\existing-solution
aspire init --language csharp
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                When the directory contains a <Highlight>.sln</Highlight> or
                <Highlight>.slnx</Highlight>, the CLI can create a project-based AppHost and add
                references to selected projects. Review the generated project references and
                resource names; they become the AppHost's handles for your existing services.
                Aspire adds an orchestration layer around your applications. It does not require
                replacing their endpoints, business logic, or normal project files.
            </p>
            <p>
                Keep onboarding repeatable: document the SDK and container-runtime prerequisites,
                the required local settings, and the command or IDE launch profile for the
                AppHost. A new teammate clones the repository, supplies local secrets, starts one
                AppHost, and uses the dashboard to inspect the whole graph instead of remembering
                a sequence of service-specific terminals. In Visual Studio or a compatible IDE,
                set the AppHost as the startup project and press F5; from a terminal, run
                <Highlight>dotnet run</Highlight> in its project directory.
            </p>
            <p>
                The AppHost is a separate process that references service projects; it is not a
                library embedded into every service. Add the database and API first, confirm they
                start together, then add UI, workers, and containers one at a time. The
                <Highlight>WithReference</Highlight> and <Highlight>WaitFor</Highlight> calls in
                the next section connect resource configuration and startup readiness.
            </p>

            <h4>Optional: Create the catalog sample used in this walkthrough</h4>
            <p>
                If you do not already have an application to wire, create this small example in a
                separate workspace. Allow roughly 45–75 minutes plus image download time. Choose
                the Blazor and Minimal API starter, use standard localhost URLs, keep HTTPS enabled,
                and leave its test project for the companion article.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-powershell">
dotnet dev-certs https --trust
aspire new aspire-starter --version 13.6.5 -n Store -o Store
Set-Location Store
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                The trust command works on Windows and macOS. On Linux, follow the platform-specific
                steps in Microsoft's
                <a class="underline" href="https://learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core/security/enforcing-ssl#trust-the-aspnet-core-https-development-certificate">HTTPS development-certificate guide</a>
                and ensure both .NET clients and your browser trust the certificate before using
                the HTTPS endpoints.
            </p>
            <p>
                The sample files in this article are reader-created; no runnable demo project is
                shipped here. This walkthrough uses <Highlight>Aspire 13.6.5</Highlight> so the
                generated project layout matches the steps below. The <Highlight>AppHost</Highlight>
                describes the application,
                <Highlight>ServiceDefaults</Highlight> shares telemetry, health, discovery, and resilience
                configuration, the <Highlight>API service</Highlight> serves HTTP endpoints, and
                the <Highlight>web project</Highlight> renders the UI.
            </p>
            <h4>Add the database integrations</h4>
            <p>Run this command from the AppHost project directory:</p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-powershell">
dotnet add package Aspire.Hosting.PostgreSQL --version 13.6.5
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>Then run this command from the API project directory:</p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-powershell">
dotnet add package Aspire.Npgsql --version 13.6.5
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Ensure the AppHost project file uses
                <Highlight>&lt;Project Sdk="Aspire.AppHost.Sdk/13.6.5"&gt;</Highlight>.
                Its references to the starter's API and web projects generate the
                <Highlight>Projects.Store_ApiService</Highlight> and <Highlight>Projects.Store_Web</Highlight>
                types below. For an existing application, use the generated types for your own projects.
            </p>

            <h4>2. Describe resources, connections, and readiness</h4>
            <p>In the AppHost, use this application model in <Highlight>AppHost.cs</Highlight>:</p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
var builder = DistributedApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

var postgres = builder.AddPostgres("postgres");
var catalogDb = postgres.AddDatabase("catalog-db");

var api = builder.AddProject&lt;Projects.Store_ApiService&gt;(
        "catalog-api", launchProfileName: "https")
    .WithReference(catalogDb)
    .WaitFor(catalogDb)
    .WithHttpHealthCheck("/health");

builder.AddProject&lt;Projects.Store_Web&gt;(
        "store-web", launchProfileName: "https")
    .WithExternalHttpEndpoints()
    .WithReference(api)
    .WaitFor(api)
    .WithHttpHealthCheck("/health");

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                <Highlight>WithReference</Highlight> supplies connection information. For a database,
                this includes a named connection string, which the existing service can read through
                its usual configuration mechanism. If existing code expects
                <Highlight>GetConnectionString("Catalog")</Highlight>, pair
                <Highlight>WithReference(catalogDb, "Catalog")</Highlight> with that key, or use
                <Highlight>AddNpgsqlDataSource("Catalog")</Highlight> when adopting the Aspire
                PostgreSQL client integration. For a service, endpoint configuration
                supports service discovery, so the existing client can use the resource name rather
                than a hard-coded local port. Aspire does not change application code to consume
                settings it never reads.
                <Highlight>WaitFor</Highlight> controls startup ordering: it waits for health checks
                when available, otherwise for the dependency's running state. A running process is
                not automatically ready to serve business requests.
            </p>
            <p>
                Preserve configuration contracts that already work. Keep secrets in the existing
                secret provider, and use AppHost endpoint references when supplying environment-
                specific URLs to a UI or identity client. For example, derive an identity callback
                URL from <Highlight>api.GetEndpoint("https")</Highlight> instead of copying a
                machine-specific port. Do not copy credentials into source files. Add
                <Highlight>ServiceDefaults</Highlight> only if you want to adopt
                its shared health, telemetry, resilience, and discovery setup. If a service has
                its own health route, configure the AppHost to check that route instead of assuming
                <Highlight>/health</Highlight>.
            </p>
            <p>
                There is deliberately no persistent data volume in this introductory model.
                The managed PostgreSQL container gives the tests a fresh database on each AppHost run.
                A developer can opt into a named volume for local convenience, but tests must not
                accidentally reuse it. Resource names such as <Highlight>catalog-api</Highlight> are identifiers
                shared by configuration, discovery, and tests; do not casually rename only one side.
                Both projects use the generated <Highlight>https</Highlight> launch profile, which
                also sets Development. This matters: the starter's first profile can be HTTP-only,
                while the browser test explicitly requests the HTTPS endpoint.
            </p>

            <h4>3. Implement a small database-backed API</h4>
            <p>
                Replace <Highlight>Store.ApiService/Program.cs</Highlight> by copying all three
                blocks below into that one file, in order. Part 1 sets up the database:
                <Highlight>AddNpgsqlDataSource</Highlight> consumes the <Highlight>catalog-db</Highlight> connection string
                supplied by the AppHost and integrates the client with health checks and telemetry.
                No password or database port belongs in source control.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using Npgsql;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
builder.AddServiceDefaults();
builder.AddNpgsqlDataSource("catalog-db");

var app = builder.Build();
app.MapDefaultEndpoints();

await using (var connection = await app.Services
    .GetRequiredService&lt;NpgsqlDataSource&gt;().OpenConnectionAsync())
{
    await using var command = connection.CreateCommand();
    command.CommandText = """
        CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS products (
            id uuid PRIMARY KEY,
            name text NOT NULL
        );
        """;
    await command.ExecuteNonQueryAsync();
}
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>Part 2 maps the database-backed read endpoint:</p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
app.MapGet("/products", async (NpgsqlDataSource db, CancellationToken ct) =&gt;
{
    var products = new List&lt;Product&gt;();
    await using var command = db.CreateCommand(
        "SELECT id, name FROM products ORDER BY name");
    await using var reader = await command.ExecuteReaderAsync(ct);
    while (await reader.ReadAsync(ct))
    {
        products.Add(new Product(reader.GetGuid(0), reader.GetString(1)));
    }
    return products;
});
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>Part 3 adds the write endpoint and starts the API:</p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
app.MapPost("/products", async (NewProduct input, NpgsqlDataSource db,
    CancellationToken ct) =&gt;
{
    if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(input.Name) || input.Name.Length &gt; 100)
    {
        return Results.BadRequest("Name must contain 1 to 100 characters.");
    }

    var product = new Product(Guid.NewGuid(), input.Name.Trim());
    await using var command = db.CreateCommand(
        "INSERT INTO products (id, name) VALUES ($1, $2)");
    command.Parameters.Add(new NpgsqlParameter { Value = product.Id });
    command.Parameters.Add(new NpgsqlParameter { Value = product.Name });
    await command.ExecuteNonQueryAsync(ct);
    return Results.Ok(product);
});

app.Run();

public sealed record NewProduct(string Name);
public sealed record Product(Guid Id, string Name);
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                The SQL values are positional parameters: their order matches
                <Highlight>$1</Highlight> and <Highlight>$2</Highlight>. Startup schema creation keeps this small sample
                self-contained; use versioned migrations and a controlled migration step for a
                production database. These unauthenticated endpoints are for an isolated local
                tutorial only. Add authentication, authorization, paging, and production-grade
                validation before exposing an application to real users.
            </p>

            <h4>4. Connect the Blazor UI through service discovery</h4>
            <p>
                In <Highlight>Store.Web/Program.cs</Highlight>, keep the generated ServiceDefaults,
                Razor component registrations, middleware, and <Highlight>MapDefaultEndpoints()</Highlight>.
                Add this named client before <Highlight>builder.Build()</Highlight>:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
builder.Services.AddHttpClient("catalog", client =&gt;
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https+http://catalog-api");
});
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Remove the starter's unused <Highlight>WeatherApiClient</Highlight> registration and
                <Highlight>Components/Pages/Weather.razor</Highlight> page, since the API no longer serves weather.
                Remove its navigation link too. Create <Highlight>Components/Pages/Catalog.razor</Highlight>
                by copying all three blocks below into one file, in order. Its named form works
                with static server-side rendering.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-razor">
@@page "/catalog"
@@using System.Net.Http.Json
@@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Forms
@@using System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations
@@inject IHttpClientFactory ClientFactory

&lt;PageTitle&gt;Catalog&lt;/PageTitle&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;Catalog&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;EditForm Model="Form" FormName="AddProduct" OnValidSubmit="CreateProduct"&gt;
    &lt;DataAnnotationsValidator /&gt;
    &lt;ValidationSummary /&gt;
    &lt;label for="product-name"&gt;Product name&lt;/label&gt;
    &lt;InputText id="product-name" @@bind-Value="Form.Name" /&gt;
    &lt;ValidationMessage For="() =&gt; Form.Name" /&gt;
    &lt;button type="submit"&gt;Add product&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/EditForm&gt;

&lt;p role="status"&gt;@@status&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul aria-label="Products"&gt;
    @@foreach (var product in products)
    {
        &lt;li&gt;@@product.Name&lt;/li&gt;
    }
&lt;/ul&gt;
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>Part 2 binds the posted form model and handles submission:</p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-razor">
@@code {
    [SupplyParameterFromForm]
    private NewProduct? form { get; set; }
    private NewProduct Form => form ??= new NewProduct();

    private Product[] products = [];
    private string? status;

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        form ??= new NewProduct();
        await LoadProducts();
    }

    private async Task CreateProduct()
    {
        using var client = ClientFactory.CreateClient("catalog");
        using var response = await client.PostAsJsonAsync("/products", Form);
        if (!response.IsSuccessStatusCode)
        {
            status = "The API rejected the product.";
            return;
        }
        var created = await response.Content.ReadFromJsonAsync&lt;Product&gt;()
            ?? throw new InvalidOperationException("The API returned no product.");
        await LoadProducts();
        form = new NewProduct();
        status = $"Added {created.Name}";
    }
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>Part 3 reloads the catalog and declares the sample models:</p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-razor">
    private async Task LoadProducts()
    {
        using var client = ClientFactory.CreateClient("catalog");
        products = await client.GetFromJsonAsync&lt;Product[]&gt;("/products")
            ?? throw new InvalidOperationException("The catalog response was null.");
    }

    public sealed class NewProduct
    {
        [Required]
        [StringLength(100)]
        public string Name { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    }

    private sealed record Product(Guid Id, string Name);
}
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                <Highlight>FormName</Highlight> and <Highlight>SupplyParameterFromForm</Highlight>
                let a static server-rendered page accept the browser's form POST without requiring
                an interactive connection. Data-annotation validation blocks empty or overlong
                names, and an API rejection is shown in the status region. The web server resolves <Highlight>catalog-api</Highlight>
                using ServiceDefaults; the browser only visits the web UI. The UI posts through the
                API and then reloads the list, so you can test the complete browser-to-database
                path without adding a separate client-side API call.
                A standalone WebAssembly or JavaScript client cannot resolve Aspire's internal
                service names directly. Give it a browser-reachable URL and appropriate CORS,
                or route requests through a backend-for-frontend.
            </p>
            <p>
                The starter's <Highlight>MapDefaultEndpoints()</Highlight> normally exposes
                <Highlight>/health</Highlight> and <Highlight>/alive</Highlight> in Development. Both AppHost health checks
                depend on those routes, so keep that environment for this sample. Readiness should
                include required dependencies; liveness should not trigger restart loops just
                because an external dependency is temporarily unavailable.
            </p>

            <h4>5. Run once and inspect the whole application</h4>
            <p>From the AppHost project directory, run:</p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-powershell">
dotnet run
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Alternatively, set the AppHost as the startup project and press F5. Follow the
                dashboard URL printed by the host. You should see PostgreSQL, the logical
                <Highlight>catalog-db</Highlight> resource, <Highlight>catalog-api</Highlight>, and <Highlight>store-web</Highlight>.
                Open the web endpoint and navigate to <Highlight>/catalog</Highlight>; the empty list is expected.
                You can enter the path directly; the new page does not add a navigation link.
            </p>
            <p>
                To exercise persistence, use the API's actual HTTPS endpoint from the dashboard:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-powershell">
$api = Read-Host "Paste the catalog-api HTTPS endpoint from the dashboard"
Invoke-RestMethod "$api/products" -Method Post -ContentType "application/json" -Body '{"name":"Notebook"}'
Invoke-RestMethod "$api/products"
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Refresh <Highlight>/catalog</Highlight> and find Notebook. Explore structured logs, metrics,
                and traces for the web-to-API call. If the API is unhealthy, check the database
                and API logs before changing timeouts. First-run container pulls can take longer
                than a warm restart. The dashboard is diagnostic infrastructure; do not expose
                its authenticated development session publicly.
            </p>
            <p>
                Stop the AppHost with Ctrl+C before starting the companion tests. This local sample
                uses synthetic data and no cloud services; there is no cloud-resource charge, but
                image downloads, storage, and your container-runtime license may have costs.
                When the AppHost removes its temporary container, its sample database is discarded.
            </p>

            <h4>6. Grow the model without growing the startup checklist</h4>
            <p>
                A larger system might add an identity service, workers, Kafka, and existing images.
                For example, install <Highlight>Aspire.Hosting.Kafka</Highlight> on the AppHost at the same
                compatible Aspire version, then add a broker before <Highlight>Build()</Highlight>:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
var events = builder.AddKafka("events");
api.WithReference(events).WaitFor(events);
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                This adds the broker and supplies connection configuration. It does
                <Highlight>not</Highlight> publish messages or create a consumer in your API.
                Add the corresponding client integration and application behavior deliberately.
                For an existing Docker image, use <Highlight>AddContainer</Highlight>, define its endpoints
                and health check, and supply configuration in the shape that image expects.
                Register a worker with <Highlight>AddProject</Highlight> and reference only the resources
                it actually needs.
            </p>
            <p>
                Keep database initialization separate from service readiness, avoid dependency
                cycles, and remember that a resource count includes supporting containers and
                logical resources, not just independently deployed services. Thirty resources
                are manageable because the connections are explicit, not because Aspire removes
                all distributed-system complexity.
            </p>
            <p>
                Code can also live in multiple repositories. An AppHost can reference projects
                from a controlled checkout layout or orchestrate prebuilt, versioned images.
                Aspire does not fetch private repositories, build arbitrary missing code, or
                guarantee compatible versions for you. The next article tests this single-repository
                sample and explains how to extend its assertions across repository boundaries.
            </p>

            <h4>References</h4>
            <ul>
                <li><a class="underline" href="https://aspire.dev/get-started/first-app/">Build your first Aspire app</a></li>
                <li><a class="underline" href="https://aspire.dev/get-started/add-aspire-existing-app/">Add Aspire to an existing application</a></li>
                <li><a class="underline" href="https://aspire.dev/get-started/csharp-service-defaults/">C# ServiceDefaults</a></li>
                <li><a class="underline" href="https://aspire.dev/integrations/databases/postgres/postgres-get-started/">PostgreSQL hosting and client integrations</a></li>
            </ul>
        </How>

        <Summary>
            <ul>
                <li>The AppHost declares the API, PostgreSQL database, UI, and their dependencies.</li>
                <li>Add Aspire to an existing application incrementally; a new starter is not required.</li>
                <li><Highlight>WithReference</Highlight> supplies service configuration; <Highlight>WaitFor</Highlight> coordinates startup.</li>
                <li>Health checks help establish readiness, while an HTTP request verifies business behavior.</li>
                <li>Service discovery keeps internal service addresses out of application code.</li>
                <li>Keep local orchestration separate from production deployment and protect real data.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                <strong>What to read next:</strong> continue with
                <a class="underline" href="/blogs/distributed-testing-with-aspire-and-playwright-in-dotnet/">distributed testing with Aspire and Playwright</a>
                to verify the API, database, and UI as one system.
            </p>
        </Summary>
    </ContentBody>
</Content>
